  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nOur entire CWA will be much cooler today as post-cold frontal air \ncontinues to push east into WI. Low stratus will also characterize \nmost of today, limiting daytime heating. Highs today will only reach \nthe low to mid 50s across western and central MN while areas to the \nsouth and east will see low to mid 60s. The stratus will begin to \nbreak apart as we progress through Tuesday night. Overnight lows \nwill be chilly for western and central MN with mid to upper 30s \nexpected. To the areas south and east, low to mid 40s are forecast. \nTemperatures will stabilize Wednesday into Thursday with highs in \nthe mid 50s to lower 60s. The chilly overnight lows of mid to upper \n30s will be more widespread over the northern half of the CWA \nWednesday night.\n\nOur next opportunity for rain arrives during Thursday morning as a \ncutoff low over the central Rockies moves northeast into the Central \nPlains. The rain shield north of the low's warm front will spread \nnortheast into southern MN and west-central WI during Thursday. \nMeanwhile, an upper-level trough coming out of the western Canadian \nPrairies within the northern jetstream will dig southeast into the \nNorthern Plains. Forecast models show the cutoff low and trough \neventually phasing together, but likely not until reaching the Lower \nGreat Lakes region. Thus, the odds of western and central MN \nreceiving rainfall Thursday look very low. Overall, places that do \nreceive rain shouldn't expect a soaker, but 0.25\" to perhaps 0.5\" is \npossible. After the trough moves east, it is forecast to amplify \ninto the Ohio Valley. In turn, this will set up northerly flow over \nthe Upper Midwest bringing a brief cold spell to end the work week. \nLow to mid 50s highs are forecast Friday, but more importantly most \nof the CWA could see sub-freezing lows Friday night. The potential \nfor a hard freeze will need to be monitored, especially by those \nwith sensitive vegetation outside.\n\nGuidance suggests that the upper-level pattern will remain \nprogressive as large-scale ridging moves east into the north-central \nCONUS, kicking the trough east also. Temperatures will rebound \nstarting Saturday with mid to upper 60s (and possibly even some low \n70s) possible by Sunday. Long-range guidance favors ridging across \nthe Northern Plains to continue into mid-next week, greatly limiting \nrain chances and keeping temperatures above normal.",
